---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import { celestial, scenes } from '../../config/images';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Birth Chart Basics', href: '/explore/birth-chart-basics/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Birth Chart Basics — how a natal chart works"
  description="A birth chart is a map of the sky at your moment of birth. Learn what it contains — planets, signs, houses, aspects — and how astrologers read it, in plain language."
  path="/explore/birth-chart-basics/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Start here</p>
      <h1>Birth chart basics</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A birth chart — or natal chart — is a map of the sky calculated for the exact date, time,
        and place you were born. Everything else in astrology builds on it.
      </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.birthChartBasics.src}
        srcset={scenes.birthChartBasics.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={scenes.birthChartBasics.alt}
        width={scenes.birthChartBasics.width}
        height={scenes.birthChartBasics.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>What a chart actually is</h2>
      <p>
        Stand where you were born, at the moment you were born, and photograph the whole sky —
        including the half below the horizon. Note where every planet sits against the zodiac, and
        which part of the sky was rising in the east. That snapshot, drawn as a circular map, is
        your birth chart. It is entirely astronomical in its data and entirely interpretive in its
        use: astrologers read the arrangement as a portrait of your patterns, capacities, and
        seasons.
      </p>
      <h2>The four building blocks</h2>
      <p>Every chart combines the same four vocabularies:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/explore/planets/">Planets</a> are drives</strong> — the "what" of the
          chart. The Moon is your need for safety; Mars is your will to act; Saturn is your
          drive to master. Everyone has all ten.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/explore/signs/">Signs</a> are styles</strong> — the "how." A planet in
          Aries acts directly; the same planet in Pisces acts by feel. Signs color, they don't
          cause.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/explore/houses/">Houses</a> are arenas</strong> — the "where." The
          twelve houses map areas of life: identity, money, communication, home, creativity, work,
          partnership, and so on.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/explore/aspects/">Aspects</a> are relationships</strong> — the
          "how they interact." Angles between planets show which drives cooperate, which argue,
          and which fuse.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        One sentence holds the whole grammar: <em>a planet (drive) in a sign (style) in a house
        (arena), aspecting other planets (relationships)</em>. Every chart interpretation you'll
        ever hear is that sentence, elaborated.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.galaxyTidepool} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Why your birth time matters</h2>
      <p>
        The rising sign — the zodiac sign on the eastern horizon at your birth — anchors the
        twelve houses, and it changes roughly every two hours. Two people born the same day in the
        same city, hours apart, share most planet-in-sign placements but live them in completely
        different arenas. This is why astrologers ask for your exact birth time, and why a birth
        certificate beats family memory. No time?
        <a href="/birth-time/">There are honest workarounds, and it is more findable than people
        expect.</a>
      </p>
      <h2>What a chart is not</h2>
      <p>
        A chart is not a verdict, a fortune, or a personality prison. It describes tendencies and
        seasons — the raw material and the weather — never the choices you'll make with them.
        Identical charts belong to different lives, because charts don't live lives. People do.
      </p>
      <h2>Where to go next</h2>
      <p>
        Click through the <a href="/tools/explore-your-chart/">interactive chart explorer</a> to
        see the wheel in action, or start with <a href="/explore/planets/">the planets</a> — the
        chart's cast of characters.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Want this explained through your chart?"
    body="A natal reading is exactly that: your chart, your patterns, your questions — ninety minutes, recorded."
    buttonLabel="About the Natal Reading"
    buttonHref="/readings/natal/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
